# Class: <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NCBITaxon_58933> (Triticum spelta)

AnnotationAssertion(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000114> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NCBITaxon_58933> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000122>)
AnnotationAssertion(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NCBITaxon_58933> "Spelt (*Triticum spelta; Triticum dicoccum*), also known as dinkel wheat or hulled wheat, is a species of wheat cultivated since approximately 5000 BC."@en)
AnnotationAssertion(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000117> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NCBITaxon_58933> "Damion Dooley"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000118> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NCBITaxon_58933> "spelt plant"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000119> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NCBITaxon_58933> <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spelt>)
AnnotationAssertion(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000600> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NCBITaxon_58933> "Spelt was an important staple in parts of Europe from the Bronze Age to medieval times; it now survives as a relict crop in Central Europe and northern Spain, and has also found a new market as a 'health food'.  Spelt is sometimes considered a subspecies of the closely related species common wheat (*Triticum aestivum*), in which case its botanical name is considered to be *Triticum aestivum* subsp. *spelta*. It is a hexaploid wheat, which means it has six sets of chromosomes.[wikipedia]"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NCBITaxon_58933> <http://eol.org/pages/1115243>)
AnnotationAssertion(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NCBITaxon_58933> <http://www.ars-grin.gov/cgi-bin/npgs/html/taxon.pl?406903>)
